-- Biometric identification under human oversight - the EU AI Act's
-- record-keeping and verification rules (Articles 12 and 14 of
-- Regulation (EU) 2024/1689) as admission law instead of a logging
-- subsystem and a policy document.
--
-- A quick tour of the domain, in case AI regulation is not your
-- world. From 2 August 2026, organisations operating "high-risk" AI
-- systems in the EU - and a system that identifies people from
-- camera footage against a reference database is the statute's first
-- example - must keep automatic records of every use: when each use
-- started and ended, which database was checked, which inputs
-- matched, and which humans verified the results. And before anyone
-- acts on an identification, the law demands that at least two
-- people, separately, confirm it. The fines for getting this wrong
-- run to fifteen million euros or three percent of worldwide
-- turnover.
--
-- The usual answer is a logging pipeline beside the AI system, and a
-- policy document asking everyone to follow the rules. The answer
-- here is different: the record is governed at the moment of entry.
-- An identification decision without two distinct verifications is
-- not flagged afterwards by a dashboard - it cannot be committed at
-- all. A use period cannot be closed to a moment earlier than a
-- match it already produced, so the record cannot be quietly
-- shortened to hide what happened. The statutory log is not a
-- subsystem bolted on: the log IS the governed record, read back.
--
-- One idea carries the whole model. The AI system's output enters
-- the record as a claim with no standing - a candidate, not a
-- conclusion. Standing is granted only by verification under live,
-- revocable human authority. Decisions gate on standing, never on
-- the raw output. And revoking an overseer's authority stops their
-- future verifications while leaving every past decision a valid
-- record of what was decided then - because whether a decision was
-- allowed is settled when it is made, and stays settled.
--
-- There is deliberately no clock in this model. Every timestamp is
-- supplied by the proposer and judged by the rules; nothing reads
-- "now" from the machine it happens to run on. That is what makes
-- the record deterministic: replay it next year, or in front of a
-- regulator, and every admission decision comes out the same.

program biometric_identification_oversight

-- The vocabulary. Article 12(3) very nearly dictates it: the
-- deployed system and the model version in service, the assignment
-- of human oversight (Article 26(2)), the period of each use, the
-- reference database checked, the match the search produced, the
-- humans who verified it, and the decision that followed.
predicate DeployedSystem(system: Subject, deployer: Subject)
-- One service window per version - declared on the predicate: two
-- windows would mean two competing answers to "was this version
-- assessed when it ran?".
predicate VersionInService(system: Subject, version: Subject, in_service_from: Timestamp, in_service_to: Timestamp)
    unique by (system, version)
predicate OversightAssigned(person: Subject, system: Subject)
-- Who may SPEAK as a verifier, as opposed to who is allowed to
-- verify. Two different questions, and the rule below about two
-- distinct people quietly assumes an answer to the first one.
--
-- Every proposal reaches the record through some application, and
-- that application names the actor. The runtime records which
-- database login vouched for the name, so the log reads honestly:
-- "this gateway said Dr Chen did it". But if a single gateway may
-- say ANY name, then one operator with one connection can sign as
-- Chen and then as Okafor, and the two-person rule below is
-- satisfied by one person typing twice. The rule would still pass.
-- It would simply mean nothing.
--
-- These two claims close that. The first ARMS a verifier - once it
-- is admitted, that name may no longer be asserted by just anyone.
-- The second grants one database login the right to speak for one
-- person. They are separate on purpose: if the grants alone did the
-- arming, then withdrawing someone's last grant - the thing you do
-- the moment you suspect trouble - would hand their name back to
-- everybody. Withdrawing the last grant instead locks the name; to
-- return it to the open, you retract the arming claim, and that is
-- its own act on the record.
--
-- The runtime recognises these two by name and shape. What it can
-- prove is narrow and worth saying plainly: that the proposal
-- arrived over a connection authorised for that name. Not that the
-- person was present, and not that a compromised application with
-- the runtime's own database credentials could not write to the
-- tables directly.
predicate ActorAssertionRestricted(actor: Subject)
    append only
predicate ActorAssertionAuthority(actor: Subject, login_role: Subject)
predicate UseStarted(use: Subject, system: Subject, version: Subject, reference_db: Subject, started_at: Timestamp)
predicate UseEnded(use: Subject, ended_at: Timestamp)
    unique by (use)
-- The statutory records are append only, and the model now SAYS so:
-- no transformation can retract a recorded match, a verification, or
-- a decision. The runtime refuses the retract statement at authoring
-- time, so the permanence the two-person rule below leans on is a
-- declared, machine-checked commitment - not a comment.
predicate MatchRecorded(match: Subject, use: Subject, input_ref: Subject, candidate: Subject, matched_at: Timestamp)
    append only
-- One verification record per verifier per match: the same person
-- confirming twice is one voice, not two - without this, the
-- two-person rule could be satisfied by one enthusiastic colleague.
predicate MatchVerified(match: Subject, verifier: Subject, verified_at: Timestamp)
    unique by (match, verifier)
    append only
-- One decision per match, in the strong form: the match determines
-- the whole decision record - id, outcome, moment, decider. (The
-- earlier hand-written rule pinned only the id; an id carrying two
-- outcomes is a state no record should hold, so declaring the
-- discipline deliberately strengthens the model.)
predicate IdentificationDecision(decision: Subject, match: Subject, outcome: Subject, decided_at: Timestamp, decided_by: Subject)
    unique by (match)
    append only

-- Article 12(3)(a) asks for the period of each use - start and end
-- date and time. Derived, never stored: the period is computed from
-- the start and end records, so it can never disagree with them.
predicate UsePeriod(use: Subject, started_at: Timestamp, ended_at: Timestamp, length: Duration)

-- Messages to the outside world after a change commits: the
-- oversight team learns a match is waiting for human eyes; the case
-- system learns a decision was taken.
intent MatchAwaitingVerification(match: Subject)
intent IdentificationDecided(decision: Subject)

-- The statute's central condition, given its name once. Article 14(5)
-- demands verification by at least two distinct people *before* any
-- action is taken on a match. The unbound names v1 and v2 ask the
-- runtime to find witnesses - two verification records whose verifiers
-- differ and whose instants precede the moment in question. The gate
-- that admits a decision and the permanent rule over the record both
-- call this by name, so the two can never quietly drift apart - and a
-- refusal names the condition in the statute's own terms.
define two_distinct_prior_verifications(match, moment):
    MatchVerified(match, v1, t1)
    and MatchVerified(match, v2, t2)
    and v1 != v2
    and t1 at_or_before moment
    and t2 at_or_before moment

-- The bookkeeping truths. If any change would leave the record
-- violating one of these, the whole change is rejected.

-- A match belongs inside its use: never before the use started.
invariant match_after_use_start:
    MatchRecorded(m, u, _, _, at) implies (UseStarted(u, _, _, _, started) and started at_or_before at)

-- And once the use has an end, never after it. This is the quiet
-- centrepiece: you cannot close a use period to a moment earlier
-- than a match it already produced. Backdating the end of a use to
-- exclude an awkward match is not forbidden by policy - it is
-- uncommittable.
invariant use_cannot_end_before_its_matches:
    MatchRecorded(m, u, _, _, at) and UseEnded(u, ended) implies at at_or_before ended

-- Article 14(5), as a permanent property of the record: every
-- decision rests on at least two verifications by distinct people,
-- both of which happened at or before the decision. The "before"
-- half is load-bearing: the statute requires verification *before*
-- action, not merely that two verification records exist by the time
-- someone files the decision. The condition itself is declared once
-- above; this rule holds it over all admitted state, forever. That is
-- safe as an invariant (not just a gate) because verification records
-- are DECLARED append only on the predicate itself: the runtime
-- refuses any transformation that would retract one, so a decision
-- admitted under two prior verifications keeps them forever. The
-- *authority* check lives in the gates below, never here - so
-- revoking an overseer never reaches back into history.
invariant decision_rests_on_two_distinct_prior_verifications:
    IdentificationDecision(d, m, _, decided_at, _) implies two_distinct_prior_verifications(m, decided_at)

-- The only ways the record may change. Gates police the moment of
-- each action; the records then stand on their own.

transformation deploy_system(system, deployer):
    require not DeployedSystem(system, _)
    admit DeployedSystem(system, deployer)

-- A model version enters service for a window - the period its
-- assessment covers. The window is the unit of accountability: every
-- use binds the version it ran under, so "which model produced this
-- match?" is always one lookup.
transformation place_version_in_service(system, version, in_service_from, in_service_to):
    require DeployedSystem(system, _)
    require in_service_from strictly_before in_service_to
    require not VersionInService(system, version, _, _)
    admit VersionInService(system, version, in_service_from, in_service_to)

-- Article 26(2): oversight is assigned to named people - and
-- revoked. Grant and retract, not validity windows: a lapsed
-- training certificate or a role change is a revocation event, and
-- the whole point is that revocation governs the future only.
transformation assign_oversight(person, system):
    require DeployedSystem(system, _)
    require not OversightAssigned(person, system)
    admit OversightAssigned(person, system)

transformation revoke_oversight(person, system):
    require OversightAssigned(person, system)
    retract OversightAssigned(person, system)

-- Enrolment: binding a verifier's name to the logins that may speak
-- it. Whoever performs these acts is the trust root - they decide
-- whose name means whom - so the gate demands the deployer, the same
-- authority that put the system on the record in the first place.
--
-- The arming claim is append only: a name, once tied to particular
-- logins, cannot quietly drift back to open. Grants come and go
-- around it as staff and services change.
transformation restrict_verifier_identity(person, system):
    require DeployedSystem(system, actor)
    require not ActorAssertionRestricted(person)
    admit ActorAssertionRestricted(person)

transformation authorise_verifier_login(person, login_role, system):
    require DeployedSystem(system, actor)
    require ActorAssertionRestricted(person)
    require not ActorAssertionAuthority(person, login_role)
    admit ActorAssertionAuthority(person, login_role)

-- Withdrawing a login's right to speak for someone. What this does
-- NOT do is return the name to the open: with no grants left, the
-- name simply cannot be asserted at all. That is the safe direction
-- to fail in, and it is why the arming claim is separate.
transformation withdraw_verifier_login(person, login_role, system):
    require DeployedSystem(system, actor)
    require ActorAssertionAuthority(person, login_role)
    retract ActorAssertionAuthority(person, login_role)

-- A use may start only under a version in service at that instant.
-- An unassessed model cannot put anything on the record at all - not
-- as a violation caught later, but as a proposal that never commits.
-- The use binds its version here, once: a use is one continuous
-- operation of one assessed version, which is why the rules below
-- need only ask "inside this use?".
transformation start_use(use, system, version, reference_db, started_at):
    require (VersionInService(system, version, f, t) and f at_or_before started_at and started_at at_or_before t)
    require not UseStarted(use, _, _, _, _)
    admit UseStarted(use, system, version, reference_db, started_at)

-- The AI system's raw output enters the record - and only the
-- deployed system's own identity may put it there. `require actor =
-- system` binds the proposer to the system bound from the use: a
-- match attributed to this system genuinely originated from it, not
-- from an analyst typing one in. A machine actor passes through the
-- same gates as a human one - the runtime does not care whose hand
-- is on the action, only whether the record is admissible - and here
-- the actor's identity is itself part of what makes it admissible.
-- What it admits is a candidate with no standing; no decision can
-- rest on it yet.
transformation record_match(match, use, input_ref, candidate, matched_at):
    bind UseStarted(use, system, version, _, started_at)
    require actor = system
    require started_at at_or_before matched_at
    require not UseEnded(use, _)
    require not MatchRecorded(match, _, _, _, _)
    admit MatchRecorded(match, use, input_ref, candidate, matched_at)
    emit MatchAwaitingVerification(match)

transformation end_use(use, ended_at):
    bind UseStarted(use, _, _, _, started_at)
    require started_at at_or_before ended_at
    require not UseEnded(use, _)
    admit UseEnded(use, ended_at)

-- A human verification - admissible only while the verifier holds
-- live oversight for this system. The authority check is a gate, not
-- an invariant, and that placement is the doctrine: it governs what
-- may be done NEXT. Yesterday's verification under yesterday's
-- authority stays a valid record even after today's revocation.
transformation verify_match(match, verified_at):
    bind MatchRecorded(match, use, _, _, matched_at)
    bind UseStarted(use, system, _, _, _)
    require OversightAssigned(actor, system)
    require not MatchVerified(match, actor, _)
    require matched_at at_or_before verified_at
    admit MatchVerified(match, actor, verified_at)

-- The decision. Two distinct verifications that both precede it, or
-- nothing - and the decider must themselves hold live oversight:
-- acting on an identification is the most consequential step in the
-- room, and Article 26(2)'s competence-and-authority requirement
-- reaches it. The gate calls the named condition declared above -
-- the same one the permanent rule enforces - so the moment of
-- admission and the standing record can never disagree about what
-- "verified by two people first" means. When this is refused, the
-- explanation names the condition and the missing verification in
-- the statute's own terms.
transformation decide_on_identification(decision, match, outcome, decided_at):
    bind MatchRecorded(match, use, _, _, _)
    bind UseStarted(use, system, _, _, _)
    require OversightAssigned(actor, system)
    require two_distinct_prior_verifications(match, decided_at)
    require not IdentificationDecision(_, match, _, _, _)
    admit IdentificationDecision(decision, match, outcome, decided_at, actor)
    emit IdentificationDecided(decision)

-- Article 12(3)(a), computed rather than stored: the period of each
-- completed use, with its exact length. The start and end instants
-- ride along as keys, so the row reads like the statute's own log
-- line: use, start, end, duration.
derived UsePeriod(use, started_at, ended_at):
    over UseStarted(use, _, _, _, started_at) and UseEnded(use, ended_at)
    value length = ended_at - started_at
